// A condition as proof. An `if` tells the compiler what is true inside
// it, and, when a branch always leaves, what is true after it, so a
// construction the condition makes safe needs no `attempt` and can't
// fail. Here the condition doesn't raise anything: it chooses between two
// answers, and each is safe on its own path.

/// Twice the order, as a party often asks, when one order can hold that
/// many; otherwise the order as it was, and the party places a second.
action double_if_room(count: CakeCount) -> CakeCount {
    if (count <= 12) {
        // Inside the branch the compiler knows `count` is at most 12, and
        // a bound on `count` carries through arithmetic, so `count * 2`
        // is at most 24 here. `CakeCount(count * 2)` is plain, and an
        // `attempt` on it would be an error. Only a condition proves
        // this: a variable's starting value, an `assert` or a call to
        // your own function never does.
        return CakeCount(count * 2)
    }
    return count
}
